# Content-addressed files keep their committed bytes on every checkout.
# The cook stamps record a hash of each authored source and of each cooked
# output, and the stale check re-hashes the working-tree bytes: a checkout
# that rewrites line endings (Git for Windows' core.autocrlf default)
# would change the hash of every glTF and report every prop stale.
# tools/check_content_attributes.py holds every tracked file with one of
# these suffixes to `text` unset; add a suffix here and there together.

# Authored sources the stamps hash.
*.gltf -text
*.glb binary
*.bin binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.tga binary
*.dds binary
*.ktx2 binary
*.wav binary
*.ogg binary
*.mp3 binary
*.ttf binary

# Cooked outputs and their sidecars.
*.mesh binary
*.hull binary
*.anim binary
*.skel binary
*.cookstamp -text
*.checksum -text
*.meta -text

# Authored documents whose suffix names the kind, not the format (the
# asset type table's convention). They hold JSON today, so tell GitHub and
# every editor that reads .gitattributes how to highlight them; the format
# can change here without touching a single reference. These are ordinary
# text files: nothing hashes their bytes, so they must NOT be listed above.
*.scene linguist-language=JSON
*.prefab linguist-language=JSON
*.mat linguist-language=JSON
*.animctrl linguist-language=JSON
*.manifest linguist-language=JSON
*.meta linguist-language=JSON
